 "text": "**Monzo for 16–17s is one of the strongest first accounts for most 16-year-olds in the UK**, with Starling and Nationwide as excellent alternatives depending on what matters most to you.\n\nAll the main options are free, come with a debit card, have no arranged overdraft (safer while you’re learning), and are FSCS-protected (up to £120,000). At 16 you can usually open them yourself.\n\n### Top pick for most people: Monzo (16–17s)\n- Open completely on your own in the app (photo ID, your own UK mobile number, and email).\n- Excellent app with real-time notifications, spending insights, bill-splitting, and Pots for budgeting.\n- Instant Access Savings Pot pays **2.80% AER** (variable), paid monthly, with instant access.\n- Age-restricted spending (e.g. gambling) is blocked.\n- Fee-free card spending abroad (they pass on the Mastercard rate).\n- Automatically becomes a full Monzo current account at 18.\n- Which? recommended provider with high customer scores.\n\nIt’s designed for independence and building good habits without fees or debt traps.\n\n### Strong app alternative: Starling Teen Account\n- Also free and open independently in the app (passport or similar ID + proof of address).\n- Often scores highest for customer satisfaction (Which? around 86%).\n- Strong app features: instant notifications, Spending Insights, and Spaces to organise money.\n- Blocks age-restricted merchants and converts to a normal Starling account at 18.\n- Current account balance itself currently pays **0% interest** (check if separate savings options are available to you).\n\nGreat if pure app quality and support matter more than interest.\n\n### Best for interest + branches: Nationwide FlexOne\n- Free current account paying **2% AER** on balances up to £1,000.\n- Linked FlexOne Saver pays **5.00% AER** (variable) on up to £5,000 — one of the better rates available to teens.\n- App, online, phone, and branches.\n- Fee-free overseas card use.\n- You can keep the account until age 23.\n- Which? customer score around 84%.\n\nChoose this if you have money to save or prefer the option of walking into a branch.\n\n### Other options worth a look\n- **TSB Under 19s**: **2.50% AER** (variable) on balances up to £2,500 (paid monthly). Driving-related discounts appear later.\n- **NatWest Adapt**: Around 1.60% interest; sometimes has sign-up offers (e.g. gift cards). Can often be opened independently at 16–17.\n\n### Quick advice\n- Want the easiest modern start and useful tools? Go **Monzo**.\n- Want the highest customer ratings and a clean app? **Starling**.\n- Want the best interest on savings or branch access? **Nationwide FlexOne + Saver**.\n\nYou’ll typically need photo ID (passport is simplest for the app banks) and proof of address. Rates are variable and change, so double-check the bank’s site before applying. At 18 these accounts usually convert smoothly, and if you go to university you can later look at student accounts (which often include interest-free overdrafts).",
